WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.100
Microsoft.Identity.Web helps integrate ASP.NET Core web apps and web APIs with the Microsoft identity platform.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:23.560
It builds on ASP.NET Core authentication and authorization patterns so developers can configure sign-in, token validation, authorization policies, and downstream API access with maintained components rather than hand-built protocol code.

3
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.160
Web apps and web APIs have different responsibilities.

4
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:36.820
A web app may need interactive sign-in, sign-out, session behavior, token acquisition for downstream APIs, and user-facing error handling.

5
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:46.920
A web API needs bearer token validation, issuer and audience checks, authorization policy enforcement, and predictable responses for clients that call it.

6
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.120
Middleware order and endpoint authorization should be reviewable.

7
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.900
Authentication must run before authorization decisions are enforced.

8
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:04.120
Sensitive controllers, Razor Pages, minimal API endpoints, hub endpoints, and administrative routes should have clear policy requirements.

9
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:10.860
A route should not be exposed because one area of the application was configured carefully while another was left to defaults.

10
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.980
Configuration should be environment-appropriate and should not embed secrets in source control.

11
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:28.280
Tenant IDs, client IDs, authority settings, audiences, callback paths, downstream API definitions, and credential locations should be reviewed per environment.

12
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.940
Development settings should not silently become production behavior.

13
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:39.780
Token caches, downstream API calls, and incremental consent need careful review.

14
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.000
A cache should match the hosting model and data protection expectations.

15
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.260
Downstream calls should request only the permissions needed.

16
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:57.600
Authentication failure behavior should be predictable and should avoid exposing unnecessary internal details to users or API clients.
